@page "/learn/browser-options"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Reconnection settings in C# · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Reconnection settings in C#</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your scanners lose Wi-Fi for a few seconds. How do you make Blazor retry every 2 seconds?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Interactive Server · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> <code>BrowserOptions</code>, Blazor's start-up settings as a typed C# object.
        .NET 10 could already set the same reconnection options, but only in JavaScript, by starting Blazor yourself
        with <code>Blazor.start()</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a warehouse pick list. Only the list runs on the server, over a connection. When the connection drops,
        Blazor tries to rejoin. Its default is 30 tries: 10 at once with no wait, then 10 five seconds apart, then 10
        thirty seconds apart. Scanners that drop out for a few seconds want something steadier: a try every 2 seconds,
        20 times.
    </p>
    <p>
        This page asks for that the .NET 10 way, in a hand-written <code>Blazor.start()</code> script, and makes the
        kind of slip nothing catches: two option names are slightly wrong. A second version, <b>Settings from
        config</b>, spells them right but reads the values from configuration, where one of them was never set.
    </p>
    <p>
        The banner that counts the tries is hidden while the connection is up. It appears, in yellow above the list,
        the moment the connection drops, and times the waits between tries.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="Settings in a Blazor.start() script" Url="demo/browser-options/pick-list/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: Blazor.start() in JavaScript" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>Pick</b> on a line, then <b>Drop my Wi-Fi for 10 seconds</b>. The yellow banner appears, jumps to
            "11 of 30" and reads "measured waits: 0 s ×10, then 5 s ×1": Blazor's defaults, not 2 seconds and 20
            tries, and no warning anywhere. <b>Sent to the browser</b> shows the script; its names aren't the ones
            Blazor reads (see the box below). Then choose <b>Settings from config</b>. <b>Sent to the browser</b> now
            shows the line <code>maxRetries: ,</code> with no number in it. Blazor didn't start, and <b>Pick</b> does
            nothing.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Nothing checks a JavaScript object</h2>
    <p>Here's what the page sent, beside the names Blazor reads:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The script, and what Blazor made of it</span>
        <pre class="snip">Blazor.start({ circuit: { reconnectionOptions: {
    <span class="err">maxRetry</span>: 20,            <span class="dim">// Blazor reads maxRetries</span>
    <span class="err">retryIntervalMs</span>: 2000     <span class="dim">// Blazor reads retryIntervalMilliseconds</span>
} } });                    <span class="err">both ignored: the defaults apply</span>

<span class="dim">// Settings from config: Razor pastes in a value nobody set</span>
maxRetries: ,              <span class="err">SyntaxError: Blazor never starts</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The options are a plain JavaScript object, so a misspelt name is just an extra property that nothing reads. And
        because <code>autostart</code> is off, a script that won't parse means Blazor never starts at all. The page
        looks fine until someone clicks.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Put the settings in C#</h2>
    <p>Build a <code>BrowserOptions</code>, and hand it to the page with <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code>.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">&lt;ConfigureBrowser Options="warehouseWifi" /&gt;</span>

static readonly BrowserOptions warehouseWifi = new()
{
    InteractiveServer =
    {
        <span class="hl">ReconnectionMaxRetries = 20,</span>
        <span class="hl">ReconnectionRetryInterval = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2),</span>
    },
};</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        A misspelt name now doesn't compile. A value left out is <code>null</code>, which keeps Blazor's default. The
        server writes the options into the page as a <code>&lt;!--Blazor-Configuration:…--&gt;</code> comment, and
        <code>blazor.web.js</code> applies them as it starts. There's no <code>Blazor.start()</code> to write. Here's
        the same pick list with the settings in C#.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same pick list, settings in C#" Url="demo/browser-options/pick-list"
                Label=".NET 11: BrowserOptions in C#">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>Pick</b> on a line, then <b>Drop my Wi-Fi for 10 seconds</b>. The yellow banner appears and reads
            "Rejoin attempt 1 of 20". With each try its count of waits grows, from "measured waits: 2 s ×1" to
            "2 s ×2" to "2 s ×3": a try every 2 seconds. After 10 seconds it rejoins, and your pick is still there. <b>Sent to the browser</b> shows the C# names camelCased, and the
            <code>TimeSpan</code> as milliseconds.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here are the two pages:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/Reconnect/PickList.razor" Feature="browser-options">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's page. The settings are a <code>static readonly</code> object, built once, and only this page gets
            them.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/Reconnect/PickListNet10.razor" Feature="browser-options">
        <Caption>
            For comparison, not the feature: Fig. 1's page, and you can skim it. It is the whole HTML document, because
            <code>Blazor.start()</code> needs <code>autostart="false"</code> on the script tag. The highlighted block is
            the old way: that script tag, then the two scripts from the box above. The nonce, the <code>From</code>
            parameter and the in-memory configuration are only there to run the demo.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Start Blazor yourself</h2>
    <p>A .NET 10 build tried three versions of the same script, and dropped the connection each time:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three Blazor.start() scripts in .NET 10, and what the browser did</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">maxRetries: 20,
retryIntervalMilliseconds: 2000</pre>
                <span class="why">"1 of 20, 2 of 20…", trying again in 2 seconds</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ works, if every name is right</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="err">maxRetry</span>: 20,
<span class="err">retryIntervalMs</span>: 2000</pre>
                <span class="why">"11 of 30" after a second, then trying again in 5 seconds</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the defaults, with no warning</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">maxRetries:
  @@Config["Reconnect:Tries"],</pre>
                <span class="why">renders <code>maxRetries: ,</code>: "SyntaxError: Unexpected token ','"</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ Blazor never starts</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        Even the script that worked cost something. You turned off autostart in <code>App.razor</code>, kept settings
        in JavaScript where nothing checks them, and let a strict Content-Security-Policy allow an inline script.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>Typed settings, written into the page</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 the same settings are C# properties. Set them for one page with <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code>,
        as Fig. 2 does, or for every page with <code>WithBrowserOptions</code> in <code>Program.cs</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Configuration stops being a trap too. The demos don't run this case, but there's no script for Razor to paste
        a missing value into: read it as <code>int?</code>, and a missing key is <code>null</code>, which keeps Blazor's
        default.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Settings from config, in .NET 11</span>
        <pre class="snip">ReconnectionMaxRetries = <span class="hl">config.GetValue&lt;int?&gt;("Reconnect:Tries")</span>,  <span class="dim">// not set: null, Blazor's default</span></pre>
    </div>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Don't mix the two, at least in RC2.</b> This guide runs on .NET 11 RC2, a release candidate, so this may change by the final release. In RC2, once <code>WithBrowserOptions</code> is set, <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code> writes into the app-wide options, so one visit to a page would change every page, for everyone.</li>
        <li><b>Only pages with an interactive component get them.</b> The settings are written just before the page's first Server, WebAssembly or Auto component.</li>
        <li><b>Callbacks still need JavaScript.</b> A retry-interval function, <code>configureSignalR</code> and a custom <code>reconnectionHandler</code> can't be serialized, so they stay in <code>Blazor.start()</code> or a JS initializer.</li>
        <li><b>Hub settings live on the server.</b> For the hub's own options, use <code>AddInteractiveServerRenderMode(o =&gt; o.ConfigureConnection = …)</code>, also new in .NET 11.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
